= Diffusion Language Models in the Reservoir World <ch:diesn>

In this chapter, we present the architecture and operational principles of *DiESN* (_Diffusion Echo State Network_), the novel sequence modeling architecture developed in this thesis.  Specifically, we eliminate the computationally demanding, quadratic-complexity ($cal(O)(L^2)$) self-attention mechanism by replacing it with fixed, non-trainable recurrent layers inspired by Echo State Networks, instantiated through the *ParalESN* formulation. By combining these untrained diagonal recurrent reservoirs with lightweight local depthwise convolutions, modern adaptive normalizations, and an associative scan parallelization, DiESN achieves comprehensive bidirectional sequence mixing with sub-quadratic temporal scaling and a substantially reduced trainable parameter footprint.


== Model Architecture <sec:model_arch>

The overall organization of the DiESN architecture is illustrated in @fig:diesn_arch. The model processes continuous input sequences generated by the diffusion corruption process and is conditioned at every level on the current noise level (timestep).

A continuous sequence of soft-masked token representations and the scalar diffusion noise level $sigma_t$ are first mapped into the latent space by the token embedding and timestep embedder modules, respectively. The resulting sequence of embeddings $bold(u)^((0))$ is processed through a deep stack of $N$ identical DiESN blocks. Each block carries out global bidirectional sequence mixing and local feature refinement conditioned on the timestep vector $bold(c)$. 

Following the $N$ reservoir-based blocks, a single Flash Linear Attention layer performs a global cross-positional refinement step, after which an adaptive-layer-normalized linear readout head projects the hidden states to unnormalized vocabulary logits.

